  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "gpt-6-sol",
  "run": 2,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "lindroth-eva",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B040",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks at the magic wavelength"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B019",
    "B025"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. The awardable core is not incremental clock improvement but the physical solution of magic-wavelength confinement: many atoms can interrogate a narrow optical transition without the dominant trapping perturbation. That idea, followed by decisive experimental development, has produced clock systems whose systematic effects are understood through demanding comparisons between independent laboratories. Their use in fundamental measurements is important evidence of maturity, but the basic quantum-control achievement is itself sufficient. Katori and Ye remain the most coherent two-person attribution for the originating principle and its major realization and development, provided the final historical review confirms that this scope does not absorb the wider optical-clock field.",
    "case_against": "The remaining reservation is substantial but focused. Optical-frequency metrology, neutral-atom clocks, and later lattice-clock performance advances involved important contributors beyond the proposed pair. A final decision should verify priority for the magic-wavelength lattice-clock principle and identify precisely which experimental advances establish Ye's central role. The citation must not imply credit for all optical clocks, every later accuracy improvement, or downstream applications. B019 remains conceptually profound, but its two named phenomena have distinct histories; B025 is mature quantum metrology, yet its proposed achievement and attribution are less sharply bounded here.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that the direct testability of systematic-shift control, replication, and the tight connection between the magic-wavelength mechanism and the Katori-Ye attribution distinguish B040 from more diffuse platforms.",
        "response": "I agree, especially on the evidential value of independent comparisons. In precision atomic physics, an extraordinary reported uncertainty is not alone decisive; agreement among separately developed systems indicates that the relevant atomic and environmental shifts have been identified and controlled. This reinforces B040's maturity without turning its practical performance into the object of the prize."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "Olsson maintains that aberration-corrected TEM is a transformative, decades-proven instrumental invention, but identifies unresolved division of credit among theory, engineering, practical correction, and alternative schemes.",
        "response": "I agree with the force and durability of B061. It also usefully sets the correct standard for B040: enabling instruments deserve recognition only when the original physical advance and individual contributions can be delimited. On the supplied record, the lattice-clock mechanism provides a narrower awardable boundary and the proposed two names a more readily testable chain from principle to realization; archival scrutiny remains necessary for both cases."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig regards KPZ universality as a clean theory-to-experiment achievement but questions whether the allocation between theoretical originators and experimental confirmation is sufficiently secure.",
        "response": "I share the attraction of a universal physical law confirmed experimentally. The same caution about exact credit leads me to retain B040: its experimental maturity is distributed across many replications, while the proposed citation can remain confined to a distinct quantum-control principle and its decisive development. For KPZ, the supplied account leaves a harder judgment about whether one experimental representative appropriately completes the theoretical achievement."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether historical evidence establishes that Katori and Ye adequately represent the original magic-wavelength lattice-clock concept and decisive realization without excluding an essential co-originator.",
      "How narrowly the final citation must be framed to distinguish optical lattice clocks from the broader development of optical-frequency metrology and later performance improvements."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct personal or collaborative connection to the named candidates is apparent from the supplied profile. My work in atomic and attosecond physics makes close attention to the scientific case natural, but it does not create an identified candidate-specific conflict."
  }
}
